    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_bdafee89",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "stereotype_of_young_men_raising_hands_as_defiant_or_proud",
      "label": "Stereotype: Young men raising hands as a sign of pride or defiance",
      "description": "The gesture of raising hands in unison is often culturally coded as a sign of celebration, rebellion, or pride, especially in contexts like sports, protests, or group identity. In memes, this gesture is frequently used to imply a group's collective identity or behavior, which can be interpreted as aggressive, boastful, or overly confident. Understanding this stereotype is necessary to interpret the meme's sarcastic tone and its implied judgment of the group's 'pride'.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_1891af7e"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_569e31e4",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "stereotype_of_young_men_as_unreliable_or_problematic",
      "label": "Stereotype: Young men as unruly, untrustworthy, or socially undesirable",
      "description": "The meme's text, 'if this is what they call pride.. then i don't wanna see what they call shame,' relies on a broader cultural stereotype that associates young men (especially in certain social or racial contexts) with negative traits like recklessness, criminality, or lack of responsibility. This stereotype is used to frame the group's 'pride' as so extreme or offensive that it makes 'shame' seem even more undesirable — a rhetorical device that implies the group is so bad that their 'shame' would be worse. This requires external knowledge of how society often stereotypes young men in this way.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_1891af7e"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_1eb7fe81",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "stereotype_of_young_men_as_uneducated_or_unskilled",
      "label": "Stereotype: Young men as uneducated or lacking in social skills",
      "description": "The meme's tone suggests contempt for the group's perceived lack of sophistication or social awareness. This stereotype is often used to dismiss or belittle groups perceived as 'simple' or 'unrefined'. The phrase 'then i don't wanna see what they call shame' implies that the group's 'pride' is so crude or embarrassing that their 'shame' would be even more so — a rhetorical device that relies on the stereotype that young men (or certain groups) are not capable of nuanced behavior or social decorum.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_1891af7e"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_ced1c312",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "stereotype_of_young_men_as_untrustworthy_or_unreliable",
      "label": "Stereotype: Young men as untrustworthy or unreliable",
      "description": "The meme's sarcastic tone and the phrase 'then i don't wanna see what they call shame' imply that the group's 'pride' is so extreme or inappropriate that it makes 'shame' seem even more undesirable. This relies on the stereotype that young men (or certain groups) are inherently untrustworthy or unreliable, making their 'pride' seem even more dangerous or offensive. This stereotype is often used to justify negative judgments or dismissive attitudes toward certain groups.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_1891af7e"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_927a45a9",
